 ## Overview
-This method integrates real-time incompressible fluid simulation with sparse voxel-based rasterization on graphics hardware, specifically leveraging Direct3D geometry shaders for rendering and compute shaders for simulation [S1]. It solves the problem of efficiently visualizing fluid dynamics within a voxel engine by bypassing CPU-GPU data transfer bottlenecks through a GPU-only pipeline [S1]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by utilizing a specific optimization where voxel activation logic and color ramping are moved entirely into the geometry shader, using a dedicated texture of non-zero values to drive stream-out, distinguishing it from [Voxel-based 3D Fire Simulation](3411-voxel-based-3d-fire-simulation.md) which may use different emission models, and [Niagara Fluids Volume Rendering](7739-niagara-fluids-volume-rendering.md) which operates within a different engine architecture [S1].
+This method integrates real-time incompressible fluid simulation with sparse voxel-based rasterization on graphics hardware, specifically using Direct3D geometry shaders for rendering and compute shaders for simulation [S1]. It solves the problem of efficiently mapping the continuous velocity and pressure fields of a Navier–Stokes solver to a discrete voxel grid for rendering, avoiding the performance bottleneck of copying simulation data back to the CPU [S1]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by moving the voxel activation and color ramp logic entirely into the geometry shader, utilizing stream-out for deterministic primitive ordering, and relying on the Jacobi method for pressure projection due to its GPU efficiency [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-The input is a 3D spatial domain defined by a chunk size $(N_x, N_y, N_z)$, typically a cube such as $64 \times 64 \times 64$ [S1]. The output is a rendered voxel scene representing the fluid field [S1].
+*   **Input:** A 3D simulation domain (chunk) defined by grid dimensions (e.g., $N \times N \times N$). External forces (static or dynamic bodies) and initial velocity/pressure fields.
+*   **Output:** A rendered voxel scene representing the fluid, where active voxels are determined by non-zero velocity/pressure values, colored via a ramp function.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-The simulation relies on GPU compute textures composed of single-precision float 4 types (`float4`) [S1].
-*   **Velocity Textures:** Three textures store the velocity field (current, previous, and temporary) [S1].
-*   **Pressure Textures:** Two textures store pressure values (current and temporary) [S1].
-*   **Speed Grid Texture:** The final result of the five GPU compute stages is a 3D speed grid texture filled with single-precision floats, serving as the source fluid field for the rendering engine [S1].
-*   **Non-Zero Texture:** A specialized texture containing only values that do not equal zero, used to drive the geometry shader for voxel activation [S1].
-*   **Staging Texture:** Used only in the CPU approach to transfer data from GPU memory to CPU memory; it must match the size of the 3D voxel staging texture [S1].
+*   **Velocity Textures:** Three 3D textures of type `float4` (single precision) storing velocity components and temporary values for advection and diffusion [S1].
+*   **Pressure Textures:** Two 3D textures of type `float4` storing pressure values and temporary values for the projection step [S1].
+*   **Speed Grid Texture:** The final 3D texture filled with single-precision floats representing the updated velocity field, which serves as the source for the voxel engine [S1].
+*   **Activation Texture:** A 3D texture containing only non-zero values from the simulation, used by the geometry shader to determine voxel presence [S1].
+*   **Staging Texture (CPU Approach only):** A texture used to transfer data from GPU memory to CPU memory [S1].
+*   **Vertex Buffer:** A cached buffer storing generated vertex data via stream-out [S1].
 
